See README for further details about Quackle, its licensing, and its
copyright.

Quackle was first ported to Windows by John Fultz, jfultz@wolfram.com, who
is also the original author of this ReadMe.

The cmake build steps documented in [README.md](README.md) work fine under Windows with
Visual C++.  Quackle used to build with mingw, and perhaps it can still be made to
do so, but that configuration is no longer supported.

A note about using cmake under Windows...cmake is finicky about backslashes.  Specify
all pathnames (e.g., in the CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH) with forward slashes (/).

For Qt 6.12 installed at C:/Qt/6.12.0/msvc2022_64, the quacker CMake project
automatically detects Qt. For another installation, set QUACKLE_QT_ROOT or
CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH to the Qt installation prefix in Visual Studio's CMake settings.

Additional things to know:
--------------------------
* To build the Quackle installer,
  + Run a cmake build in quacker\build
  + Build the RelWithDebInfo build
  + Copy any needed DLLs into build\RelWithDebInfo
  + From the Quackle directory, run the following...
    <full path to Inno Setup's ISCC.exe> installer.iss /FQuackleInstaller /O. /Q
  + Find the file QuackleInstaller.exe in the quackle\ directory.

To create a ZIP of the built Windows application, run PowerShell from the repository root:

    .\scripts\package-windows.ps1

By default, the script packages the `quacker-release` preset's build directory,
`out\build\quacker-release`, into `out\Quackle-Windows-x64.zip`. It includes the
executable, deployed Qt DLLs and plugins, license, `README.Quackle.txt`, and runtime
data; it does not include source archives. To use a different build directory or
output file:

    .\scripts\package-windows.ps1 -BuildDirectory out\build\quackle-vs-qt-discovery -OutputPath out\Quackle.zip

Build and deploy the application first so `Quackle.exe`, its DLLs, and Qt platform
plugins are present in the selected build directory.

To publish an existing ZIP as a GitHub Release, authenticate with Git Credential
Manager, commit the source changes, build that commit, and create the ZIP. Then run:

    .\scripts\publish-windows-release.ps1 -Tag v1.2.0 -Notes "Windows release"

Use a new tag. The script tags the current commit, pushes it to `origin`, creates a
release draft, uploads the ZIP, and publishes the release. Use `-WhatIf` to preview
or `-Prerelease` to mark a prerelease. `-ZipPath`, `-Title`, and `-Notes` are optional.
The script does not build the application or verify that the ZIP matches the commit;
untracked files are not included in the tag. If publishing fails after pushing the tag,
check the tag and release draft on GitHub before retrying. GitHub automatically shows
source-code archives for releases; these cannot be removed by this script.
